{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791563858,"nonce":"Al3nzBQqie9lfoKA3pffUYiOwfQ=","title":"Lua: each load() builds and compiles its chunk natively before running it","message":"`load_chunk` hands every chunk to `TieredRuntime::join_typed_program`, which lowers the typed program through the shared pipeline (imports, checking, SSA, interpreter-safe optimisations) and compiles every function it declares at the Cranelift baseline before the chunk runs: \"the piece is small and about to run, and the interpreter may be busy running its caller\". A program that loads many small chunks and runs each once pays the native compile for every one; lua5.4 interprets them.\n\nofficial/constructs.lua with the conformance prelude, at 2b4657e6eadf20120b8703127002640f22108ee9 on the Mac (release, LLVM unselected, ZYNTAX_TRACE_LOWER_PHASES, measured while a cargo test ran beside it): 6774 loads, 21 s wall. load:compile (join_typed_program) 20.31 s, mean 3.00 ms per chunk; load:lower (Lua frontend) 1.01 s, 0.149 ms; load:init 0.16 s; load:parse 0.12 s. Per-1000-load medians of load:compile rise 2.29, 2.22, 2.40, 2.84, 3.06, 3.06, 3.74 ms, so some per-load cost still grows with what is loaded (not yet attributed; the run shared the machine). A 10 s sample split join_typed_program into compile_module_entered (CraneliftBackend::compile_module about 20% of load time) and lower_typed_program (about 19%).\n\nTargets: interpret a joined chunk first where the caller's interpreter state allows it, or make the join pipeline's per-chunk work proportional to the chunk; find what still grows per load. Keep the error-flag, entry and debug behaviour of loaded chunks.","files":null}]}